@@ -0,0 +1,264 @@
+# 🛠️ Updated Rule: Adding GitHub Environment Variables to the Codebase (Enhanced)
+
+> **Purpose**: This rule ensures GitHub environment variables are correctly implemented across Terraform, Python, and CI/CD workflows, avoiding incomplete or incorrect configurations.
+
+---
+
+## 📌 1. **Define Terraform Variables in a Dedicated `variables.tf`**
+
+### ✅ **Requiremenßts**
+
+- Create a new or update an existing `variables.tf` file in the relevant module directory (e.g., `terraform/resources--services/variables.tf`).
+- Use the `variable` block with `type`, `description`, and `default` (optional) for clarity and consistency.
+
+### 📜 Example
+
+```hcl
+variable "aws_wif_role" {
+  type        = string
+  description = "AWS WIF role ARN for cross-account access."
+  default     = ""
+}
+
+variable "aws_wif_region" {
+  type        = string
+  description = "AWS WIF region for cross-account access."
+  default     = ""
+}
+```
+
+### ⚠️ **Why This Matters**
+
+- Ensures variables are explicitly declared and accessible to Terraform.
+- Avoids assumptions about variable locations (e.g., not relying on global `variables.tf`).
+
+---
+
+## 📌 2. **Set Variables in GitHub Actions Workflow**
+
+### ✅ **Requirements**
+
+- Add `TF_VAR_<VAR_NAME>`-prefixed environment variables to the `env` block of the relevant job (e.g., `deploy_resources_services`).
+- Use GitHub secrets (`${{ secrets.ENV_VAR_NAME }}`) or vars (`${{ vars.ENV_VAR_NAME }}`) to inject values securely.
+
+### 📜 Example
+
+```yaml
+jobs:
+  deploy_resources_services:
+    env:
+      TF_VAR_aws_wif_role: "${{ secrets.AWS_WIF_ROLE }}"
+      TF_VAR_aws_wif_region: "${{ secrets.AWS_WIF_REGION }}"∂
+```
+
+### ⚠️ **Why This Matters**
+
+- Ensures Terraform receives variables as inputs during deployment.
+- Secures sensitive values using GitHub secrets (not hardcoded in workflows).
+
+---
+
+## 📌 3. **Integrate Variables into Terraform Modules**
+
+### ✅ **Requirements**
+
+- Use `environment_variables` in modules or `google_cloud_run_v2_service` resources to pass variables to Cloud Run services.
+- Use `concat` for flexibility when combining existing and new variables.
+
+### 📜 Example (Module Integration)
+
+```hcl
+module "results" {
+  source = "git::ssh://git@github.com/CroudTech/dst-terraform-module-cloud-run-microservice.git?ref=tags/v1.1.1"
+  environment_variables = concat([
+    {
+      name  = "SHARED_SA_EMAIL"
+      value = google_service_account.shared_service_account.email
+    },
+    {
+      name  = "IMPERSONATE_FOR_AWS_WIF"
+      value = local.impersonate_for_aws_wif
+    },
+    {
+      name  = "AWS_WIF_ROLE"
+      value = var.aws_wif_role
+    },
+    {
+      name  = "AWS_WIF_REGION"
+      value = var.aws_wif_region
+    }
+  ])
+}
+```
+
+### Ⓢ Example (Direct Resource Definition)
+
+```hcl
+resource "google_cloud_run_v2_service" "ai" {
+  name        = local.ai_service_name
+  template {
+    containers {
+      env {
+        name  = "SHARED_SA_EMAIL"
+        value = google_service_account.shared_service_account.email
+      }
+      env {
+        name  = "AWS_WIF_ROLE"
+        value = var.aws_wif_role
+      }
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+### ⚠️ **Why This Matters**
+
+- Ensures variables are dynamically injected into services.
+- Avoids hardcoding values in Terraform configurations.
+
+---
+
+## 📌 4. **Create a Python Config Class for the Variable**
+
+### ✅ **Requirements**
+
+- Update `constants/constants.py` with a new `_<VAR_NAME>Config` class.
+- Define `EnvVar` or `EnvBooleanVar` for each variable.
+
+### 📜 Example
+
+```python
+class _AWSWIFConfig:
+    AWS_WIF_ROLE_VAR = "AWS_WIF_ROLE"
+    AWS_WIF_REGION_VAR = "AWS_WIF_REGION"
+    AWS_WIF_ROLE: EnvVar = EnvVar(name=AWS_WIF_ROLE_VAR)
+    AWS_WIF_REGION: EnvVar = EnvVar(name=AWS_WIF_REGION_VAR)
+```
+
+### ⚠️ **Why This Matters**
+
+- Centralizes variable access in Python code.
+- Ensures type safety and consistency across the codebase.
+
+---
+
+## 📌 5. **Update Code to Use the New Config**
+
+### ✅ **Requirements**
+
+- Import the config class and access variables via `CONFIG.<CLASS_NAME>.<VAR_NAME>`.
+
+### 📜 Example
+
+```python
+from seomax.constants.constants import CONFIG
+
+# Use variables
+aws_wif_role = CONFIG.AWS_WIF. AWS_WIF_ROLE
+aws_wif_region = CONFIG.AWS_WIF. AWS_WIF_REGION
+```
+
+### ⚠️ **Why This Matters**
+
+- Decouples code from raw environment variables.
+- Improves readability and maintainability.
+
+---
+
+## 📌 6. **Create Required Terraform Resources**
+
+### ✅ **Requirements**
+
+- If the variable depends on a Terraform resource (e.g., `SHARED_SA_EMAIL`), define the resource explicitly.
+
+### 📜 Example
+
+```hcl
+resource "google_service_account" "shared_service_account" {
+  account_id   = "sa-${var.environment}-shared-service-account"
+  display_name = "Shared Service Account"
+  description  = "Service account that can be impersonated by other service accounts"
+}
+```
+
+### ⚠️ **Why This Matters**
+
+- Ensures variables depend on valid resources.
+- Avoids undefined values in environment variables.
+
+---
+
+## 📌 7. **Define Local Variables for Internal Use**
+
+### ✅ **Requirements**
+
+- Define local variables in `locals.tf` for reuse across services.
+
+### 📜 Example
+
+```hcl
+locals {
+  service_accounts_to_impersonate_shared_account = [
+    module.sa_scraper_service.email,
+    module.sa_validator_service.email,
+    # ... other service accounts ...
+  ]
+  impersonate_for_aws_wif = true
+}
+```
+
+### ⚠️ **Why This Matters**
+
+- Reduces duplication and improves maintainability.
+- Ensures consistency across services.
+
+---
+
+## 📌 8. **Expose Resource Outputs for Reuse**
+
+### ✅ **Requirements**
+
+- Add outputs to expose resource properties (e.g., `shared_service_account.email`).
+
+### 📜 Example
+
+```hcl
+output "shared_service_account_email" {
+  value       = google_service_account.shared_service_account.email
+  description = "Email of shared service account."
+}
+```
+
+### ⚠️ **Why This Matters**
+
+- Enables reuse of resource values in other modules or services.
+- Facilitates debugging and testing.
+
+---
+
+## ✅ Final Checklist
+
+Before finalizing the change, ensure all the following are addressed:
+
+- [ ] Variables defined in a dedicated `variables.tf`.
+- [ ] Variables injected via `TF_VAR_<VAR_NAME>` in GitHub workflows.
+- [ ] Variables integrated into Terraform modules or resources.
+- [ ] Python config class created in `constants/constants.py`.
+- [ ] Code updated to use the new config.
+- [ ] Required Terraform resources defined.
+- [ ] Local variables defined in `locals.tf`.
+- [ ] Outputs added for resource properties.
+
+---
+
+## 📌 Summary
+
+This rule ensures GitHub environment variables are implemented **completely**, **securely**, and **maintainably**. It avoids the mistakes from previous attempts by explicitly requiring:
+
+1. Proper variable declaration in Terraform.
+2. Secure injection via GitHub secrets.
+3. Integration into modules and resources.
+4. Python config classes for consistency.
+5. Local variables and outputs for reusability.
+
+By following this rule, you’ll avoid incomplete or incorrect configurations and ensure your infrastructure is robust and scalable.
